I’ll start from the specimen metadata and diffraction pattern, then locate the frozen reference/indexing and GSAS-II workflows in the provided baseline. I’ll prioritize phase closure and distinct structural topologies, and I’ll checkpoint the first complete physical multiphase model before testing optional alternatives.

The chemistry points first to Mn–W–O products plus possible Mn₃O₄/WO₃ carryover, but I won’t assume a single product. I’m now extracting the peak families and querying the frozen COD index for exact Mn–W–O and precursor references; this will also reveal whether multiple polymorph/topology branches need explicit testing.

The first complete model is now established and checkpointed: wolframite MnWO₄, trigonal Mn₃WO₆, and monoclinic WO₃ account for every coherent peak family. The minor phases also satisfy the 1:1 Mn:W feed balance: two moles of residual WO₃ per mole of Mn₃WO₆ compensate that phase’s Mn-rich stoichiometry. The initial three-phase refinement gives Rwp ≈ 7.12%; remaining misfit is mainly peak shape and relative intensity, not an unindexed family. I’m preserving this physical replay before comparing WO₃ topology alternatives and testing whether Mn₃O₄ has any stable residual scale.

The topology screen is running in parallel for monoclinic, triclinic, orthorhombic, tetragonal, hexagonal, and the alternate monoclinic WO₃ cells, all against the same MnWO₄ + Mn₃WO₆ state and the same staged calibration/profile relaxation. The current physical branch has already separated a plausible ~800 µm specimen displacement from a bounded zero offset and indicates strongly phase-dependent broadening, especially a very small Mn₃WO₆ domain size and strained WO₃.

The branch jobs are still refining rather than stalled; each includes full-grid cell/calibration and phase-specific size/microstrain stages, so the six-way comparison is compute-heavy. Once they finish, I’ll only carry coordinate refinements forward for topologies that reproduce the observed WO₃ splitting and pass geometry checks.

Three concurrent branches are likely contending for the available CPU, so this comparison is taking longer than the single-model refinements. I’m letting the already-launched jobs finish to keep their stages comparable; no additional searches are being opened while the indexed three-phase model remains complete.

The six jobs are still active with no error output because each branch writes its own log. Their runtime is consistent with several 18–24-cycle full-pattern refinements under CPU contention. I’ll inspect convergence and discard any numerically failed branch only after the processes return; failure alone would be recorded as unknown, not used to reject a topology.

The screen remains within a reasonable runtime for 18 refinements over 8,301 points. The main model is safely checkpointed, so even if a branch later proves numerically unusable, the accepted physical replay is preserved and no result is at risk.

This is now clearly the dominant cost of the analysis, but it is bounded: six branches, three stages each. I’m continuing rather than interrupting mid-refinement, since comparable completed checkpoints are more informative than partial Rwp values and the user requested topology coverage.

The branch runner still has no terminal output by design because logs are isolated per topology. If it exceeds the expected cycle budget materially, I’ll stop the batch and retain completed branch checkpoints rather than let a pathological candidate consume the rest of the analysis.

The completed screen rejects the high-symmetry WO₃ branches on peak topology, not just Rwp: orthorhombic, tetragonal, hexagonal, and the alternate monoclinic cell require boundary broadening/calibration or scale collapse and cannot reproduce the observed triplet/splitting. Two low-symmetry branches remain genuinely close—P2₁/c monoclinic (Rwp 6.91%) and P−1 triclinic (6.87%)—so I’m carrying both through identical coordinate and intensity refinement and will choose on geometry/stability rather than the 0.04% early Rwp difference.

Both surviving WO₃ branches are now in the coordinate sequence: all metal positions first, then oxygen coordinates phase-by-phase with a saved CIF/GPX after every step. This makes any intensity gain auditable and lets me roll back an individual light-atom stage if bond geometry becomes implausible.

The coordinate jobs remain active. The triclinic branch has roughly twice as many independent WO₃ atom coordinates as the monoclinic branch, so I’ll judge its eventual Rwp gain against displacement magnitudes and W–O geometry rather than rewarding extra degrees of freedom automatically.

The unconstrained coordinate trial did exactly what the safeguards were meant to catch: it lowered Rwp to 4.64–4.74% but created nonphysical short contacts (down to 0.28–0.34 Å in WO₃, and ~1.65 Å Mn–O). Those light-atom stages are rejected. The monoclinic heavy-atom model remained geometrically sensible; the triclinic heavy refinement did not. I’m now rerunning both from their physical profile checkpoints with explicit displacement bounds and will accept only stages that preserve coordination geometry.

The bounded rerun limits metal-site movement to 0.06 Å and oxygen-site movement to 0.08 Å from each published parent while retaining scale, cell, calibration, and profile relaxation. These bounds are tight enough to prevent the earlier coordination collapse but broad enough to accommodate modest reference/sample mismatch.

The bounded jobs are still completing the phase-by-phase oxygen stages. A parameter hitting one of these coordinate limits will be treated as weakly determined; it will not be interpreted as a reliable site displacement even if the profile improves.

Both branches continue normally under the tighter bounds. Because every stage is copied as a standalone GPX before the next one, I can select the last geometrically valid improvement rather than being forced to keep the final stage.

The bounded coordinate comparison is the final expensive branch test. After it returns, I’ll geometry-screen the checkpoints, test the plausible Mn₃O₄ precursor by scale stability, then refine only the accepted three-phase replay and generate the required deliverables.

The jobs are taking longer than the unconstrained trials because active bound handling causes additional refinement passes. They remain within the pre-set finite cycle count, so I’m allowing them to finish rather than comparing partially converged models.

No new branch or search has been opened during this wait. The workload is still exactly the two bounded low-symmetry WO₃ candidates, and the earlier complete physical checkpoint remains the fallback.

