The elapsed time now matches the prior full screen. I’m giving the two jobs one final minute to return; otherwise I’ll stop them cleanly and analyze whichever bounded phase checkpoints have already been written.

The first bounded attempt exposed a GSAS-II convention issue: coordinate variables named `dA*` are bounded as absolute fractional coordinates, not zero-centered shifts. That run therefore collapsed sites toward the origin and is rejected as a numerical setup failure, not evidence against either phase. I’ve corrected the bounds to be centered on each published coordinate and am rerunning the same two branches.

The corrected run is active with bounds centered on the actual parent coordinates. I’ll explicitly verify the saved Cartesian displacement, minimum interatomic distance, and cation coordination for every checkpoint before accepting any Rwp change.

The corrected jobs continue through their finite oxygen stages. Since the coordinate bounds are now local to each site, a boundary hit will leave the atom near its parent rather than generating the origin-collapse seen in the discarded setup.

Geometry screening identified a source-quality issue in the initially tested 1960 monoclinic WO₃ coordinates: they contain a 1.57 Å O–O contact and incomplete octahedral coordination. I’m replacing that diagnostic parent with the neutron-derived P2₁/c record 2311041, which has physical geometry and the same low-symmetry reflection topology. This is a targeted parent repair within the accepted topology, not a new phase search.

The physical neutron P2₁/c parent starts at Rwp 6.97%, comparable to the triclinic and older monoclinic fits but without the latter’s short O–O contact. Its bounded coordinate replay is now running; this is the last same-topology comparison needed before phase-model selection.

The physical low-symmetry comparison is now resolved. The neutron P2₁/c WO₃ parent preserves six-coordinate W with W–O = 1.65–2.23 Å and reaches Rwp 5.97% after bounded metal coordinates plus bounded MnWO₄ oxygen refinement. Later oxygen stages are rejected because many sites hit bounds and profile terms become nonphysical. The triclinic branch is close but offers no robust physical advantage, while P2₁/c is the room-temperature WO₃ form; I’m selecting the physical neutron P2₁/c checkpoint for final refinement.

The selected physical replay is checkpointed at Rwp 6.19% after resolving the zero/displacement singularity with an 800 µm fixed specimen displacement and a refined 0.214° zero term. Before texture/profile finishing, I’m testing tetragonal Mn₃O₄ explicitly from several starting abundances; it will only be rejected if its scale converges stably to zero or negative values.

The weak Mn₃O₄ trial converged to ~0.8–0.9 wt% from all starts, but its strongest contribution is entirely overlapped and the three-phase fractions alone close the 1:1 Mn:W balance essentially exactly. I’m treating Mn₃O₄ as a model-dependent sub‑1% possibility, not a reliably identified phase. The texture screen is now testing one phase/axis term at a time from the selected three-phase checkpoint.

The texture screen found two clear, physically bounded effects: WO₃ March–Dollase [010], r = 1.309 (Rwp 4.31%), and MnWO₄ [111], r = 1.136 when tested alone (Rwp 5.55%). Other axes were much weaker. I’m combining those two supported terms, then I’ll test Mn₃WO₆ texture only in that combined state.

The two supported textures combine cleanly and reduce Rwp to 3.18% with bounded ratios (MnWO₄ [111] = 1.144; WO₃ [010] = 1.313). I’m checking whether Mn₃WO₆ needs a third texture term; it will be retained only if the combined-state improvement is clear and axis-specific.

No Mn₃WO₆ texture axis is uniquely supported: all tested directions improve Rwp by only ~0.04% and are nearly degenerate, so I’m omitting that term. The remaining residuals show systematic low-angle peak asymmetry rather than missing reflections; I’m testing the axial-divergence term next, followed by polarization/surface roughness only if each produces a stable physical improvement.

Axial divergence did not improve the fit and is rejected. Surface roughness collapsed to its zero-effect boundary with A/B 99.98% correlated, so that family is also unsupported. Polarization is physical and modestly beneficial; I’m converging that term without roughness before testing any phase-specific anisotropic broadening.

The anisotropy screen is testing only the three uniaxial microstrain axes allowed by the specimen model, one phase at a time. Isotropic crystallite sizes are retained; no generalized strain tensor or extra profile family is being introduced.

I’m finishing the last optional anisotropic-broadening screen now. I’ll retain it only if one axis gives a distinct, physical improvement; otherwise the converged texture/polarization checkpoint remains the final model.

The screen found one clean, axis-specific broadening term: MnWO₄ microstrain along [010] lowers Rwp from 3.172% to 2.943% with both components positive and no frozen/out-of-bound parameters. The other eight trials either worsened the fit, collapsed to zero/bounds, or were axis-degenerate, so only this MnWO₄ term is defensible. I’m validating that checkpoint and its residuals before export.

The anisotropic checkpoint is now numerically converged (maximum inter-run shift <0.001σ). One remaining physically necessary check is phase-level displacement: the accepted WO₃ neutron parent carries zero thermal displacement, while the stated laboratory model permits 0.003–0.020 Å². I’m screening only a single shared Uiso per all-isotropic phase, with that physical bound; MnWO₄’s published anisotropic displacement tensors will remain untouched.

